<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  Mutation is the only source of novelty in the soup: nothing else writes bytes a program
  did not already contain. DESIGN §1.3 sweep 1 predicts a window. At
  <span class="mono">mutation_rate = 0</span> the soup can only shuffle what randomness
  handed it, so emergence is delayed; far above the error threshold — Eigen's
  quasispecies bound — a replicator's copies are corrupted faster than it can make them,
  and nothing persists. Between the two there should be a band of rates where the time to
  emergence is shortest.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  A 128×128 torus of 64-byte tapes, 20 000 epochs per run, ten mutation rates from
  <span class="mono">0</span> up to <span class="mono">2<sup>-8</sup></span>, ten seeds
  each. Every other parameter is at its default. The dependent variable is
  <em>transition epoch</em> as defined in DESIGN §1.2: the first sampled epoch at which
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> falls below 0.6 and stays below it for the next
  three samples. A run that never crosses that line is censored, not zero, and appears as
  a hollow marker along the top of the phase diagram.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  All 100 runs finished. What follows states the shape of the sweep; the per-run numbers
  behind it are in the two tables below, which read the runs live. The first of them names each
  finished run that either observable flagged — the transition detector or the
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> census — with its peak census, its lowest
  <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span>, its highest <span class="mono">copy_rate</span>
  and a CSV of its samples.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Life emerged.</strong> In a handful of seeds a random soup with no fitness
  function and no selection pressure collapsed into self-replicating structure:
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> holds near the random-soup floor for thousands of
  epochs, falls off a cliff inside a few sampling intervals, stays down for the rest of the
  run, and the snapshots of those runs show one colony spreading across the torus from that
  epoch on. Every censored run instead ends where it started, high. That is the BFF result,
  reproduced here with nothing selecting for it.
</p>

<p>
  The earliest transition of the sweep is the <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span>
  arm's first seed, and its <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> has the shape every
  transitioning run has — the random-soup plateau, a cliff, then a floor it never leaves.
</p>

<%= findings_run_chart(experiment_slug: "mutation-rate", seed: 1, metric: "compress_ratio",
                       params: { "mutation_rate" => Lab::EMERGENT_MUTATION_RATE }) %>

<p>
  <strong>The census agrees where the effect is strong.</strong> The two strongest
  transitions of the sweep — the first and the fifth seed of the
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span> arm — carry a large
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> while they are running: hundreds of
  replicating cells at the peak, with <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> falling from
  the random-soup value near 7.85 to under 5 and <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span>
  down to a fraction of the soup floor. Where the effect is strong the detector and the
  census agree, and they agree in the stored worlds too: rescoring the first seed's
  snapshot near its peak counts several hundred replicating cells and one tape holding a
  few percent of the torus and passing the 3-of-4 replicator trial. The lineage is in the
  world, not in the sampling.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The census fades; the compression does not.</strong> After the peak the count
  falls back towards zero over the following samples while
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> stays collapsed for the rest of the run — a
  lineage that spread, saturated and dissolved, leaving the compressible debris behind.
  That is why a terminal snapshot under-counts: a rescore of a run's last stored world, the
  cheapest thing to ask for, reads the world after the lineage is gone and reports none.
  The zero censuses read that way earlier in this sweep are a snapshot-retention artefact
  and not evidence that the census never sees a lineage.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Three runs still disagree with themselves.</strong> Two flagged runs — the ninth
  seed of the <span class="mono">2<sup>-12</sup></span> arm and the fifth seed of the
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-8</sup></span> arm — collapse entropy and lift
  <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> off the floor with a peak census of zero, no ranked
  tape passing the replicator test at any sample; and the second seed of the
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-8</sup></span> arm carries a census of a couple of cells with
  no flag at all. They are reported as disagreements, not resolved in either direction.
  Ranking width is not the explanation: rescoring at the top 16, 64 and 256 tapes returns
  identical metrics, so the cut stays where DESIGN §1.2 locks it, at 16.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>A floor, and no ceiling.</strong> Nothing transitioned at any rate at or below
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> — the zero-mutation control included, which the
  hypothesis expected to be slow rather than silent. Transitions appear only at
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span> and above, and in no arm more often than 2 of
  10. They are fastest in the <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span> arm: the earliest
  transitions of the sweep are there, and the arms above it transition later when they
  transition at all. The sharp collapse in <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> that marks
  a soup turning into copies of one thing is confined to the two lowest transitioning arms;
  higher up, the flagged runs stay diverse. And there is no upper error threshold anywhere
  in the swept range: the top arm transitions as often as the middle ones do.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The window is not there.</strong> A window predicts a peak — an interior band
  where emergence is fastest and common, falling away on both sides. What the sweep shows is
  a lower cutoff and then a plateau at the resolution ten seeds per arm can give: at 2 of 10
  against 0 of 10 the arms at or above <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span> are not
  distinguishable from each other. The hypothesis of DESIGN §1.3 sweep 1, as stated, is not
  supported by its own data.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The cutoff is suggested and not established.</strong> Pooling the arms at the
  split the transitions themselves suggest gives 0 of 40 runs at rates
  ≤ <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> against 5 of 60 at
  ≥ <span class="mono">2<sup>-13</sup></span>, a one-sided Fisher exact test on that 2×2
  table giving <span class="mono">p ≈ 0.073</span>. That is suggestive and nothing more —
  and the split was drawn after looking at where the transitions fell, which makes it weaker
  than the number looks.
</p>

<p>
  What the mutation rate does change, and changes unambiguously, is the floor a soup without
  replicators sits on: among the censored runs the final
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> climbs arm by arm from
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> to <span class="mono">2<sup>-8</sup></span>
  without a single reversal, while the three arms below
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span> sit in no order at all, the no-mutation arm the
  highest of them. That is mutation randomising the tapes faster than interaction can make
  them alike. It is real, it is large, and it has nothing to do with emergence.
</p>

<p>
  Three caveats. The detector fires on compression and diversity, not on a census of
  copiers, so it is a transition in the DESIGN §1.2 sense and not a count of replicators;
  the census disagrees with it on some flagged runs, in both directions, which is exactly
  what the per-run table above is for. Every 0/10 arm is censored at 20 000 epochs rather
  than negative, and several transitions landed after epoch 10 000, so an arm that shows
  nothing here may only be slower than the budget. And nothing here is a clean negative
  until the <%= link_to "positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> transitions: a
  sweep with a handful of events in a hundred runs is a weak instrument whichever way it
  points.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  Emergence at 128×128 within 20 000 epochs is rare enough that this sweep mostly measured
  censoring: 95 of 100 runs stopped before anything happened, so most of what was compared
  between arms is where the clock ran out. Turning "one seed in ten" into a rate needs
  longer runs, larger worlds, or both, and the sweeps for that are queued.
  <%= link_to "Mutation rate, long runs", experiment_path("mutation-rate-long") %> re-runs
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-14</sup></span>…<span class="mono">2<sup>-11</sup></span> for
  60 000 epochs — three times the budget — to separate "rare" from "slow" and to test the
  lower cutoff exactly where it was drawn.
  <%= link_to "World size", experiment_path("world-size") %> asks whether emergence is a
  per-world event or a per-cell rate; if it is per-cell, a bigger world is the cheapest way
  to buy events. And the
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", experiment_path("bff-control") %> — 2<sup>17</sup>
  tapes, well-mixed, 50 000 epochs — is the run that says whether this engine reaches the
  published emergence rate at all. Until it finishes, every censored run above is evidence
  about the instrument as much as about the soup.
</p>
